<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · ideology</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antisemitism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1879 CE – present</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antisemitism is hostility to or discrimination against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, rooted in ancient prejudice and Christian anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and named in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1879. Its racial form culminated in the Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> of some six million Jews, and scholars still dispute how to define it.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The German historian Reinhard Rürup described the revolutions as a turning point in the development of modern <b>antisemitism</b>, as conspiracy theories presented Jews as representing both social revolution and international capital.</blockquote><a href="/movement/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848 · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Stoecker, when his pro-worker policies failed to win the working class, turned to <b>anti-Semitism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/christian-democracy">Christian democracy · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>Martin Broszat held that almost all its essential elements were present in the radical protest movements of pre-1914 Germany: a virulent <b>antisemitism</b>, a blood-and-soil ideology, the notion of a master race and the idea of territorial acquisition and settlement in the East.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nazism">Nazism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Hitler's autobiography and manifesto, written partly in prison, set out his <b>antisemitism</b> and his aim of conquering 'living space' in the east.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-18">18 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
